-- Source URL: https://calendar.unt.edu/event/unt-physics-colloquium-presents-dr-jie-zhou
-- Location match: Physics Building, 104 -> Physics Building (PHYS) (6bd46f58-a130-4a44-9cdc-049ae5de6ea3)
-- Match reason: exact alias match on 'physics'
WITH
resolved_location AS (
    SELECT '6bd46f58-a130-4a44-9cdc-049ae5de6ea3'::uuid AS location_id
),
existing_event AS (
    SELECT e.event_id
    FROM events e
    JOIN event_details d ON d.event_id = e.event_id
    WHERE d.metadata ->> 'source_uid' = 'tag:localist.com,2008:EventInstance_52381822216856'
      AND COALESCE(d.metadata ->> 'source_recurrence_id', '') = ''
    LIMIT 1
),
updated_existing_event AS (
    UPDATE events
    SET
        title = 'UNT Physics Colloquium Presents Dr. Jie Zhou',
        description = 'Title: Semiconductor Grafting: A Scalable Approach to Lattice-Mismatched Heterojunctions

 

Abstract: Semiconductor heterojunctions have revolutionized modern electronics, optoelectronics, and photonics. Traditionally, their fabrication via epitaxy or heteroepitaxy requires strict lattice matching, limiting material choices and device architectures. While lattice-matched heterojunctions have driven significant technological progress, integrating lattice-mismatched semiconductors while preserving their crystallinity remains an unsolved challenge. Overcoming this limitation could open new frontiers in materials engineering and device innovation.

In this talk, I will introduce semiconductor grafting, a novel and practical framework for forming near-abrupt, electrically active heterojunctions between lattice-mismatched materials. This approach exploits quantum tunneling mechanisms to facilitate efficient charge transport while maintaining the intrinsic order of each semiconductor and minimizing interface defect states. I will then demonstrate the scalability of this technique by fabricating and characterizing wafer-scale (≥2-inch) grafted heterojunctions that exhibit high throughput, uniformity, and performance. Finally, I will highlight their application in advanced electronic, optoelectronic, and photonic devices, illustrating their potential for next-generation semiconductor technologies.',
        location_id = (SELECT location_id FROM resolved_location),
        start_date_time = TIMESTAMP '2026-03-24 15:30:00',
        end_date_time = TIMESTAMP '2026-03-24 16:30:00',
